From 43247f6f9b48458945a85dab5488cf9764adef7b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ayiejosh <132535860+ayiejosh@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 02:11:35 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 04/43] Revert 'pvr: stop advertising 8-bit storage/shaderInt8'
 - it was unjustified

That disable was based on static reasoning only (extract_u8/insert_u8 have no cases in
pco_trans_nir.c and trans_conv's default is UNREACHABLE). No test failed, and the bench
repo's vk16 probe - which actually exercises 8-bit storage and int8/uint8 arithmetic in a
compute shader - computes the correct values in every case:

  results: f16 1.5*2.25*8 = 27 (want 27), i8 -100/3+128 = 95 (want 95), u8 200+100 = 44 (want 44)

So the 8-bit ops that matter are emitted, and the ones I worried about are not reached.
Disabling advertised-but-working features reduces compatibility, which is the opposite of
the objective. Restored storageBuffer8BitAccess, uniformAndStorageBuffer8BitAccess,
storagePushConstant8 and shaderInt8.

vk16 goes 2 failures -> 1 -> 0 (after updating its now-stale shaderFloat16 expectation).
The shaderFloat16 = false fix STAYS: that one is evidence-backed (20/27 glmark2 scenes
render wrong with it on, 0/27 with it off, and it is worth +42.8% mean FPS).

Lesson: static absence of a case is not evidence of a bug. The bench probes exist to
settle that, and vk16 settled this one the other way.
